WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.790
Two of my classes both had a mean of

2
00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.210
twelve in the science test.

3
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.140
Same number, so same class?

4
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:08.240
No.

5
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.320
In one class everyone was close to twelve.

6
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.900
In the other, marks were

7
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.000
all over the place.

8
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.460
Today: measuring how spread out data is,

9
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.220
and spotting values that do not fit.

10
00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.870
The standard deviation is the typical

11
00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.810
distance of the values from the mean.

12
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.440
Small means the values

13
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.980
huddle close to the mean.

14
00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.730
Large means they are spread far away.

15
00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.830
To work it out, find each value's distance

16
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.130
from the mean, square it, take the mean of

17
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.450
the squares, and then the square root.

18
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.480
The computer does this in one line.

19
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.640
Your job is to read it.

20
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.560
Class A has a standard

21
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.540
deviation of about one mark.

22
00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.030
Class B has about five.

23
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.040
The same mean, but class B needs very

24
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.060
different help for different pupils.

25
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.460
An outlier is a value far from the rest.

26
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.000
A height of three hundred centimetres, or

27
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.940
a mark of ninety in a test out of twenty.

28
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.650
Outliers deserve a closer look

29
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:19.910
before anything else.

30
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.120
One simple rule uses the

31
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.400
middle half of the data.

32
00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.850
Find the quartiles, and

33
00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:27.810
the gap between them.

34
00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.090
Anything more than one and a

35
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.910
half gaps beyond the quartiles

36
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.470
is flagged as an outlier.

37
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.200
Quick check!

38
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.280
The register says one pupil took three

39
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.060
hundred minutes to get to school.

40
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.740
What should you do first?

41
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.070
Check it first.

42
00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.190
It could be a typing mistake for thirty.

43
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.440
Or it could be true, a pupil who

44
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.820
walks from a far village.

45
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.000
Never delete an outlier just

46
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.220
because it is inconvenient.

47
00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.590
In the app, open The

48
00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:00.790
middle and the spread.

49
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.040
Drag one mark far away, and

50
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.860
watch how the range and the

51
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.360
middle half react differently.

52
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:09.190
Your turn.

53
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.140
Class C has a standard deviation

54
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.060
of two, and class D of six.

55
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.590
Which class has more mixed marks?

56
00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:19.640
Class D.

57
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.080
Remember: the standard deviation is the

58
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.720
typical distance from the mean.

59
00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.430
Outliers are far from the rest.

60
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.980
Check them, explain them,

61
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.380
and never hide them.

62
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.160
Try it in class tomorrow: take last

63
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.800
term's marks without names, find the

64
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.620
mean and the standard deviation, and

65
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.580
look for outliers.

66
00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.490
Talk about what each outlier might mean.
